//# #2745: an assignment REBINDS its target, not only continues past it.
//
// Codex, P1 round 5 on #2761, written in source rather than reached through a
// lowering. Round 4 made the walk continue past an assignment to its
// CONTINUATION, which answers `{ let mut b = a; b = a; b }` because the
// INITIALIZER was already erased. It does not answer this one: the initializer
// is a call whose return type this pass cannot read, so `b` was recorded
// unknown and only the assignment makes it hold the formal. Measured, it
// printed `252` -- the tagged pointer.
//
// This fixture and `lambda_bound_erased_interp_loop_refused.vibe` are the same
// rule seen from both ends, which is what keeps the fix from being a special
// case for the lowered `__loop_result` name.
//
// The obvious spelling of that twin -- `{ let mut b = 0; b = a; b }` -- is NOT
// a program: a slot cannot hold both `Int` and an erased `T`, and the checker
// says so (`argument type mismatch for inner: expected Int, got Qt`). The gate
// rejected that fixture, which is the gate working; a compiler-generated dummy
// initializer like the loop's is exactly the case the checker never sees.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn wrap_it[U](x: U) -> U {
  x
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    "\{ { let mut b = wrap_it(a)
    b = a
    b } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
